<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julius Classicianus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 65</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Gaius Julius Alpinus Classicianus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaius Julius Alpinus Classicianus was the Roman procurator, or chief financial officer, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> appointed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a>'s revolt of 60 or 61. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, he reported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> that the harsh reprisals of the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-suetonius-paulinus">Suetonius Paulinus</a> were prolonging the conflict, which led to Paulinus' recall. His tombstone, found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-classicianus.jpg" alt="Julius Classicianus" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elliott Brown from Birmingham, United Kingdom · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tombstone_inscription_of_Classicianus_(replica)_(8108925836).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
